# How to rotate image and save the image

The core challenge you are facing isn't just about changing a CSS property; it is about the fundamental difference between visual transformation and data modification. When you use `jquery-rotate`, the library manipulates the canvas rendering of the DOM element. It calculates the rotation visually but does not alter the underlying pixel data or the original file source stored on your server. This explains why you see two images: one is the original static resource loaded from your URL, and the other is a dynamically rendered overlay or canvas that represents the rotated view. To achieve your goal of displaying only the rotated image and saving it, you must abandon client-side-only rotation in favor of re-rendering the bitmap data.

You cannot rotate an existing image file via jQuery alone because browsers treat `<img>` tags as references to external resources. They do not allow direct modification of the file bytes stored on the server through JavaScript. To get a single, true rotated image, you need to perform the rotation in memory and then save that new data stream.

The most robust approach involves using an HTML5 Canvas element combined with the `html2canvas` library. This stack allows you to capture the visual state of your image (including any CSS rotations applied via jQuery) and render it into a new raster buffer. Once rendered, you can convert that canvas back into a data URL or a Blob, which represents a complete, standalone image file.

Here is how you can structure the workflow using jQuery for the interaction and server-side code to handle the persistence:

First, update your HTML to use a canvas element instead of an `<img>` tag, or overlay a canvas that captures the rotated view. You will need `jquery-rotate` to handle the animation logic, but the final step requires capturing the result.

```html
<canvas id="cropbox" width="400" height="300"></canvas>
<input type="button" id="img" name="img" value="Rotate & Save" />
```

In your JavaScript, you maintain the rotation state and apply it to the canvas context rather than a DOM image. However, since `jquery-rotate` is designed for DOM elements, a common pattern is to rotate a hidden `<img>` element visually, capture it to a canvas, and then display that canvas. Alternatively, you can manually draw the rotated image onto the canvas every time the button is clicked.

```javascript
$(function() {
    var rotation = 0;
    var originalImage = new Image();

    originalImage.src = "demo_files/pool.jpg";

    originalImage.onload = function() {
        var canvas = document.getElementById('cropbox');
        var ctx = canvas.getContext('2d');

        $("#img").click(function() {
            rotation = (rotation + 45) % 360;

            // Clear previous drawing
            ctx.clearRect(0, 0, canvas.width, canvas.height);

            // Save context state to handle translation and rotation
            ctx.save();

            // Move origin to center of canvas for rotation
            ctx.translate(canvas.width / 2, canvas.height / 2);
            ctx.rotate(rotation * Math.PI / 180);

            // Draw the image centered
            ctx.drawImage(originalImage, -originalImage.width / 2, -originalImage.height / 2);

            // Restore context
            ctx.restore();

            // Save the rotated canvas as a new file (e.g., JPG or PNG)
            var dataURL = canvas.toDataURL('image/jpeg', 0.9);

            // You can now send this dataURL to your server via AJAX/PHP
            saveToServer(dataURL, 'rotated_image.jpg');
        });
    };
});

function saveToServer(dataURL, filename) {
    var formData = new FormData();
    formData.append('image', dataURL, filename); // Note: browser handles encoding

    // Example AJAX call (adjust based on your backend requirements)
    $.ajax({
        url: '/save-image', // PHP endpoint
        type: 'POST',
        data: formData,
        processData: false,
        contentType: false,
        success: function(response) {
            console.log('Image saved successfully');
        }
    });
}
```

On the server side, your PHP script needs to receive this data. Since `FormData` sends the image as a binary stream, you cannot simply parse it as JSON. You must read the raw input stream from the uploaded file or the data URL (if sent as base64). If sending as base64, you need to decode the string and write the binary data to a file using PHP's `file_put_contents`.

```php
// Example: Handling a POST request with a saved image
$handle = fopen($_FILES['image']['tmp_name'], 'rb');
$imageContent = '';

while (!feof($handle)) {
    $imageContent .= fread($handle, 1024);
}

fclose($handle);

file_put_contents('path/to/rotated_image.jpg', $imageContent);
```

The trade-off here is performance. Rendering to a canvas and re-saving the file requires CPU resources on every click. If your application handles high traffic, this operation should be asynchronous. Additionally, client-side rotation libraries like `jquery-rotate` are deprecated; modern alternatives or pure HTML5 Canvas implementations offer better performance and support for newer browsers without relying on external plugins that may no longer be maintained.
